records reads the daemon's store: what the decoders heard while a kept job
was running ('ley decode <name> --job' starts one). A job without --job keeps
nothing, so an empty table usually means no kept job has run.

Rows are newest first. TIME is wall clock derived from the capture's anchor,
the way every time in ley is; a record whose capture the daemon no longer has
an anchor for shows no time rather than a guessed one.

--since takes a duration: 90s, 30m, 1h, 2d. --near takes a latitude and a
longitude ('37.76,-122.42') and --radius a distance with its unit (10km, 500m,
5nm, 3mi); together they ask for records from a place.

--json prints a RecordPage: the records and the anchors that date them.

Usage:
  ley records [flags]

Examples:
  ley records                              # everything kept, newest first
  ley records --protocol aprs --since 1h   # the last hour of APRS
  ley records --device-id LEYTST-1         # one transmitter
  ley records --near 37.76,-122.42 --radius 10km
  ley records --json | jq -r '.records[].deviceId'

Flags:
      --device-id string   only this transmitter, e.g. LEYTST-1 (the id the protocol gives it, not a radio)
  -h, --help               help for records
      --in-effect          only records whose validity window covers now (alerts, warnings)
      --job-id string      only this job's records: a job id from 'ley jobs --wide'
      --kind string        only this kind of record, e.g. position, weather, status
      --limit uint32       at most this many rows, e.g. 50 (default: the daemon's 1000)
      --near string        only records from around here: LAT,LON, e.g. 37.76,-122.42 (needs --radius)
      --protocol string    only this protocol, e.g. aprs ('ley decoders' lists them)
      --radius string      how far around --near to look: 10km, 500m, 5nm, 3mi
      --since string       only records newer than this, e.g. 1h, 30m, 2d

Global Flags:
      --ascii           draw with ASCII only (no box, bar or tree glyphs)
      --color string    colour output: auto, always or never (default "auto")
      --json            machine output: proto3 JSON (NDJSON for streams)
      --socket string   daemon socket path (default: user daemon UDS, $LEYLINE_SOCKET)
